  "worked_solution": "The particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x)+3x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x)-9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n6(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x)-9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x)+9x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x)=-6\\operatorname{cos}\u20613x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}\u20613x+6\\beta \\operatorname{cos}\u20613x=-6\\operatorname{cos}\u20613x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u20613x) \\\\ -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u20613x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =0$ and $\\beta =-1.$ Therefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x\\operatorname{sin}\u20613x.\n$$",
